  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If the ratio of \\(2x-y\\) to \\(x+y\\) is \\(\\frac23,\\) what is the ratio of \\(x\\) to \\(y?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac15\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac45\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac65\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac54\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Write the given ratio as\n\\[\n\\frac{2x-y}{x+y}=\\frac23,\n\\]\nwhere \\(x+y\\ne0\\). Cross-multiplication removes the fractions:\n\\[\n3(2x-y)=2(x+y).\n\\]\nExpanding and collecting terms gives\n\\[\n6x-3y=2x+2y,\n\\qquad 4x=5y.\n\\]\nIf \\(y=0\\), this last equation would force \\(x=0\\), contradicting \\(x+y\\ne0\\). Thus we may divide by \\(4y\\), obtaining\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac54.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac54}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
